---
Use `http://127.0.0.1:8000` for the server in the
[LLMD quickstart]($link.page('llmd/quickstart')). A client that accepts an
OpenAI-compatible base URL uses `http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1`. For a remote
deployment, use its base URL and any authentication it requires.

# [List the loaded model]($heading.id('list-the-loaded-model'))

`GET /v1/models` returns the model served by this LLMD instance in `data`.
Use its `id` in chat requests. The CLI's `--model-name` sets the ID.
When omitted, LLMD uses the basename of `--model`. Start a server with a
different checkpoint to serve a different model. See
[Checkpoint storage]($link.page('llmd/quickstart').ref('checkpoint-storage'))
for model sources and downloads.

```sh
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/models
```

# [Create a chat completion]($heading.id('create-a-chat-completion'))

Send JSON to `POST /v1/chat/completions` with the ID returned by your server.
This chat example uses `LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8`.

```sh
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with one short greeting."}],
    "max_completion_tokens": 64
  }'
```

| Request field | Type and default | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | String, required | ID from `GET /v1/models`. |
| `messages` | Array, required | Conversation history in order. A text message has a `role` and `content`. The loaded model's chat template determines supported roles and content types. |
| `max_completion_tokens` | Integer, optional | Limit generated tokens. The server also enforces its context limit. The older `max_tokens` field is used when `max_completion_tokens` is absent. |
| `stream` | Boolean, `false` | Set to `true` for server-sent events. |
| `stream_options.include_usage` | Boolean, `false` | Emit an additional usage event when streaming. |
| `temperature` | Number, optional | Request-level sampling temperature. |
| `top_p` | Number, optional | Request-level nucleus sampling threshold. Use `0` through `1`. |
| `tools` | Array, optional | Offer function definitions to a tool-capable model and chat template. |

For the request-level sampling behavior documented in
[LLMD 20261112.0](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261112.0/#sampling-controls),
`temperature` and `top_p` override the CLI value, then the model recommendation
and fallback apply when needed. See the [CLI reference]($link.page('llmd/cli'))
for server defaults. Additional request fields and modalities depend on the
release and model. Verify that optional fields have the intended effect on
your release because the server can ignore unknown JSON fields.

The JSON response includes `model`, `choices`, and `usage`. Read
`choices[0].message.content` for the text and `choices[0].finish_reason` for
the reason generation ended. A typical text response includes the following
fields. The generated text and token counts vary.

```json
{
  "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Hello!"},
      "finish_reason": "stop"
    }
  ]
}
```

# [Stream a chat completion]($heading.id('stream-a-chat-completion'))

Set `stream` to `true`. With curl, `-N` displays events as they arrive.

```sh
curl -fsSN http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with one short greeting."}],
    "max_completion_tokens": 64,
    "stream": true,
    "stream_options": {"include_usage": true}
  }'
```

The response uses `text/event-stream`. Parse each `data:` event as JSON and
append text from `choices[0].delta.content` when present. A usage event has an
empty `choices` array. A completed stream ends with `data: [DONE]`. For a streaming
tool call, assemble the `delta.tool_calls` fragments by their tool-call index
before parsing the arguments.

# [Call tools from a client]($heading.id('call-tools-from-a-client'))

Use a checkpoint and chat template with
[tool calling support](https://zml.ai/llmd/). Include function definitions in
`tools`, and ask for the function in the conversation. For
example, add this field to a chat request where the user asks for the time in
Paris.

```json
{
  "tools": [
    {
      "type": "function",
      "function": {
        "name": "get_local_time",
        "description": "Return the local time in a city",
        "parameters": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {"city": {"type": "string"}},
          "required": ["city"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

When the model requests a call, read `choices[0].message.tool_calls`. Each
function call carries an `id`, a `function.name`, and `function.arguments`
encoded as a JSON string. The client validates and executes the function.
For the next completion, include the assistant message with its tool calls,
followed by a message with `role` set to `tool`, `tool_call_id` set to the
matching ID, and `content` set to the tool result.

# [Decision]($heading.id('decision'))

`POST /v1/systemone` accepts a JSON object and returns scored labels for the
requested questions. The loaded checkpoint must support Decision. Use the
[worked request]($link.page('llmd/decision').ref('send-a-decision-request'))
or copy one from the [quickstart]($link.page('llmd/quickstart').unsafeRef('send-a-decision-request')).

| Request field | Type | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `state` | String or object, required | Input shared by every question. The server renders an object as compact JSON. |
| `questions` | Object, required | Maps question IDs to definitions. IDs must be nonempty and exclude the colon character. Send dependencies before the questions that reference them. |
| `instructions` | String, optional | System instructions shared by all questions. |
| `ask` | Array of question IDs, optional | Select the questions to answer. Include every dependency of the selected questions. Omitting this field asks all questions. |
| `model` | String, optional | The endpoint uses the checkpoint already loaded by LLMD. The field can be omitted. |

Each question requires `kind` and a string `instructions`. Its `criteria`
describe the possible labels.

| `kind` | `criteria` | Successful `label` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `choice` | Required object mapping labels to descriptions. Use a string or `null` for each description. | One of the object keys. |
| `noul` | Optional object with descriptions under `"true"` and `"false"`. | The string `"true"` or `"false"`. |
| `score` | Required array of scale labels, such as `["low","medium","high"]`. | One of the supplied strings. |

Use `depends_on` to supply a list of earlier question IDs whose available
labels become context. Use `ask_if` to gate execution. Its keys are earlier
question IDs and its values are arrays of acceptable labels. Every named
question must return a label in its corresponding array for the question to run.

**Response.** `answers` maps each requested ID to a result. A successful entry
contains the winning `label`, a `scores` array, and `prompt_contribution`.
Each score gives the label, its first `token_id`, its full-vocabulary
`logprob`, and its `probability` normalized over the offered labels.
`prompt_contribution` is the total probability mass of the offered label
tokens before this normalization. `usage` totals prompt and cached tokens
across the questions that ran.

This illustrative response uses `"ask": ["animal"]` with the guide's
request. Token IDs, counts and scores depend on the tokenizer, model and run.

```json
{
  "answers": {
    "animal": {
      "label": "cat",
      "scores": [
        {"label": "cat", "token_id": 7, "logprob": -0.916291, "probability": 0.8},
        {"label": "dog", "token_id": 9, "logprob": -2.302585, "probability": 0.2}
      ],
      "prompt_contribution": 0.5
    }
  },
  "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 48, "cached_tokens": 0}
}
```

**Errors.** A false condition produces `{"skipped":true}` for that question.
A question can also return an `error` field with `prompt_too_long`,
`unsupported_chat_template`, `resource_exhausted`, or `aborted`.
Check individual entries even after HTTP 200. An unsupported
model or invalid request produces HTTP 400 with a plain-text explanation.
For `ForwardDependency`, put the referenced question earlier in the JSON
object. For `DuplicateOptionToken`, choose labels whose first tokens differ.

LLMD revision `ca575167` limits the request body to 4 MiB, accepts 1–1,024
questions, and scores 2–16 options per question. Its Decision path is enabled
for the LFM3 model implementation. See the [Decision catalog]($link.page('llmd/models').unsafeRef('decision-checkpoints'))
for checkpoint and build evidence, and the [CLI reference]($link.page('llmd/cli').ref('batching-context-and-memory'))
for the per-request concurrency control `--systemone-window`.

# [Create embeddings]($heading.id('embeddings'))

`POST /v1/embeddings` maps input text to a numeric vector using the loaded
embedding checkpoint. Choose `Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B` in the
[Embeddings catalog]($link.page('llmd/models').unsafeRef('embedding-checkpoints')).
The [quickstart]($link.page('llmd/quickstart').unsafeRef('send-an-embeddings-request'))
generates this request with the selected host port.

```sh
curl -sS --fail-with-body http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/embeddings \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "input": "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.",
    "encoding_format": "float"
  }'
```

| Request field | Type and default | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input` | String or array of strings, required | Send one nonempty string per vector. Token ID arrays are rejected. |
| `encoding_format` | String, `"float"` | Choose `"float"` for a JSON array of numbers or `"base64"` for encoded little-endian float32 bytes. |
| `dimensions` | Integer, model maximum | Sets the length of each vector. Qwen3-Embedding-8B supports 32 through 4,096 dimensions. |
| `model` | String, optional | May be omitted. LLMD embeds with the checkpoint loaded by the server. |

**Read the response.** `object` is `"list"` and `model` is the served
model ID. Each `data` entry has `object: "embedding"`, the zero-based input
`index`, and the vector in `embedding`. Entries follow input order.
`usage.prompt_tokens` and `usage.total_tokens` both count the tokens
embedded across all inputs. To inspect a float response, append
`| jq '.data[0].embedding | {dimensions: length, first_four: .[:4]}'` to
the command. See [Compare texts with embeddings]($link.page('llmd/text-similarity'))
for a client that ranks passages using the returned vectors.

**Limits and errors.** LLMD revision `fe922cff` accepts up to 2,048 strings
per request, up to 8,192 tokens per string, and up to 300,000 tokens in
total. Each tokenized string must also be shorter than the server's context
limit. An HTTP 400 response includes an `error.code`, such as
`model_not_supported` for a checkpoint without embedding support,
`invalid_dimensions` for a vector size outside the model's range, or
`too_many_tokens` for exceeding the endpoint's token limits.
A batch that LLMD cannot finish can return HTTP 503 with `resource_exhausted`.

# [Check service health and errors]($heading.id('check-service-health-and-errors'))

| Endpoint or result | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /health` | Returns `OK` from the running HTTP service. Use a chat completion to confirm inference. |
| `GET /metrics` | Exposes Prometheus metrics. See [Operations]($link.page('operations')). |
| `400 Bad Request` | Check required fields, JSON types, and the model's supported content types. |
| `400` with `error.code` set to `context_length_exceeded` | The tokenized prompt reached the server context cap. Shorten the conversation or review the server configuration. |

The examples and fields are source-reviewed. Execution against a running
LLMD instance is pending.
